        "question": {
          ":question/id": "7a977252-1271-5b40-9b88-0072e1e2f4ce",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "Let $f(x,y)=x^{3} y$. Let $C$ follow the curve $y=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1$ as $x$ goes from $e^{2}$ to $e$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t,\\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 1\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $e^{2}$ to $e$.*",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Set $x=t$ and use the Cartesian equation to obtain the other coordinate. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t,\\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 1\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $e^{2}$ to $e$. The initial point is $A=\\left(e^{2},3\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(e,2\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=\\left(e\\right)^{3} \\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=2 e^{3}\\\\\nf(A)&=\\left(e^{2}\\right)^{3} \\left(3\\right)\\\\\n&=3 e^{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(2 e^{3}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(3 e^{6}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 3 e^{6} + 2 e^{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
