        "question": {
          ":question/id": "11bc8fb4-93bb-5639-99b8-386ec8442131",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "Let $f(x,y)=2 x y + 2 y^{2}$. Let $C$ be the part of the curve $y^2=x+1$ lying above the $x$-axis, traversed from its intersection with $x=0$ to its intersection with $x=8$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The specified half-plane selects the positive values of $y$. Solving the curve equation at the two boundary lines gives the directed endpoints below. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^{2} - 1,t\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $1$ to $3$. The initial point is $A=\\left(0,1\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(8,3\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=2 \\left(8\\right) \\left(3\\right) + 2 \\left(3\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&=66\\\\\nf(A)&=2 \\left(0\\right) \\left(1\\right) + 2 \\left(1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(66\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=64.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
