          ":question/id": "7323676e-3d56-5cdf-8d0c-b1432623738a",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
          ":question/problem": "Let $f(x,y)=- x + y^{2} + y$. Let $C$ follow the curve $x=\\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$ as $y$ goes from $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to $\\pi$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r.\n$$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Set $y=t$ and use the Cartesian equation to obtain the other coordinate. A parametrization in the stated direction is $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},t\\right\\rangle$, with $t$ going from $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to $\\pi$. The initial point is $A=\\left(0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ and the terminal point is $B=\\left(-1,\\pi\\right)$. The gradient is continuous on this arc, so the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(B)&=- \\left(-1\\right) + \\left(\\pi\\right)^{2} + \\left(\\pi\\right)\\\\\n&=1 + \\pi + \\pi^{2}\\\\\nf(A)&=- \\left(0\\right) + \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^{2} + \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\frac{\\pi^{2}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $I=\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=f(B)-f(A)\\\\\n&=\\left(1 + \\pi + \\pi^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad -\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\frac{\\pi^{2}}{4}\\right)\\\\\n&=1 + \\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\frac{3 \\pi^{2}}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
